// ===================== Chenoweth · 4. Tactics, social media and the 3.5% rule =====================
m(4,"Best tactics","What kind of tactic does the chapter say seems to work very effectively to make opponents suffer immediate, direct costs?","Economic noncooperation",["Symbolic demonstrations","Lobbying of elected officials","Online petitions"],"Strikes are fairly reliable for short-term concessions; general strikes are particularly powerful; boycotts work when entire communities abide by them.",1);
m(4,"South African boycott","How did Black-led boycotts in South Africa work?","People kept working but stopped spending at white-owned shops",["People stopped working in all white-owned factories and farms","People refused to pay taxes to the apartheid government","People moved out of the townships to cut off the shops' workforce"],"After several months this forced white businesses to approach key political elites to demand reforms.",2);
m(4,"Sequencing","What sequence of tactics does the chapter recommend?","Begin with safer actions, then build up to riskier ones",["Begin with the riskiest actions to shock the regime into retreat","Use a single tactic until it fails, then switch to another","Alternate big and small demonstrations at random intervals"],"It makes movements more resilient to repression while consistently disrupting the opponent's rule.",2);
m(4,"Optimists","What do optimists say about social media and activism?","It lets activists communicate directly, quickly, and without censors",["It replaces the need for any form of lasting organization","It makes repression of mass protests practically impossible","It guarantees support from foreign governments and media"],"Social media also create a new public sphere, useful when none other exists.",2);
m(4,"Rapid mobilization","What does the chapter call perhaps the most important contribution of social media for civil resistance?","Extremely rapid mobilization",["Free publicity in the world's major newspapers","Hiding the identities of every participant","Replacing in-person meetings entirely"],"Event pages and the amplifying effects of Facebook, Twitter, WhatsApp and Instagram let people reach masses in seconds.",2);
m(4,"Causes or characteristics","How does Mary Elizabeth King describe the role of social media in recent uprisings?","As characteristics of the uprisings, not necessarily causes",["As the single main cause of the uprisings in Tunisia and Sudan","As irrelevant to every uprising of the last twenty years","As the only reason the Arab Awakenings spread across borders"],"Movements that rely solely on digital media to coordinate a revolution are quite vulnerable.",3);
m(4,"Clicktivism","What is the pessimists' concern with “clicktivism”?","A false sense of participation that reduces direct action",["Online campaigns attract far too many participants to manage","Platforms charge fees for access to political content","Clicks are used by courts as proof of political involvement"],"Skeptics argue it may actually reduce involvement in more effective forms of action.",2);
m(4,"Tufekci","According to Zeynep Tufekci, movements that want to make change first need to build…","staying power and organizational structures",["a large number of followers on several social networks","a clear digital strategy planned month by month","a network of foreign journalists ready to report"],"Digital technology makes it easy to skip building relationships, coalitions, strategies and alternative institutions.",2);
m(4,"Tactics before strategy","What temptation do social media dramatically increase, according to the chapter?","To put tactics before strategy",["To rely on the army","To avoid risk entirely","To merge with the regime"],"Movements may organize only in the short term, moving from one event to another.",2);
m(4,"Echo chambers","Why do social media make it harder for online movements to reach regime supporters?","People select comforting feeds and cluster in small cohesive groups",["Regime supporters are barred from using the platforms","Algorithms hide political content from older users","Online content is always censored before it spreads"],"Many studies find social media exacerbate the tendency to atomize into small, cohesive groups.",3);
m(4,"Anita Gohdes","What did political scientist Anita Gohdes find about activists' increasing reliance on the internet?","It made it easier for governments to track and disrupt dissent",["It made governments unable to monitor protests at all","It made protests more peaceful and better organized","It made shutdowns unnecessary because protests had ended"],"Most governments realized that tracking activists online was a smarter response than shutting down the internet.",3);
m(4,"Bahrain 2011","How did the regime use Facebook during Bahrain's failed 2011 uprising?","To organize counterprotests and intimidate protesters",["To broadcast the protesters' demands to the whole country","To negotiate openly with the opposition on reforms","To recruit new police officers from neighboring countries"],"Loyalists also used Facebook to harass and threaten protesters: the same tools help government supporters coordinate.",2);
m(4,"Sudan 2011","What did the Sudanese government do on Facebook during the failed 2011 uprising, according to Patrick Meier?","It set up mock protest pages and arrested those who showed up",["It shut down all social media for a whole year","It asked protesters to share their passwords publicly online","It paid influencers to praise the regime to young users"],"Many of those arrested were then reportedly tortured to reveal their Facebook and email passwords.",3);
m(4,"3.5% rule","What is the “3.5% rule”?","No revolution has failed once 3.5% of people joined a peak event",["Revolutions need 3.5% of the population to serve as armed fighters","Revolutions fail whenever fewer than 3.5% of people are in the streets","A movement wins once 3.5% of voters back it in a national election"],"Chenoweth coined the term during a 2013 TEDx talk.",1);
m(4,"Who uses it","Which movements have used the 3.5% figure to inspire participation?","Extinction Rebellion and South Korea's Candlelight Movement",["Otpor in Serbia and the Solidarity movement in Poland","The Mothers of the Plaza de Mayo and the Ladies Land League","The Green Belt Movement in Kenya and the Sudanese Professionals Association"],"They use it to inspire participation and to gauge capacity for change.",3);
m(4,"5% rule","What was Mark Lichbach's “5% rule” in his 1994 book The Rebel's Dilemma?","No government could withstand 5%, but free-riding keeps rebellions below 5%",["Any revolution that mobilizes under 5% of the people is bound to fail","Revolutions need 5% of soldiers to defect, and rarely win unless over 5% fight","Any government falls once 5% of its soldiers join the rebels"],"Free-riders benefit from others' risks without taking any themselves.",3);
m(4,"First caveat","What is the first caveat about the 3.5% rule?","It describes the past, not what must always happen",["It rests on future simulations rather than recorded campaigns","It applies only to armed campaigns with a clear leadership","It was disproved by the 2019 data on all campaigns"],"No one knows whether the rule will hold once people consciously try to reach the threshold without the organizing earlier movements did.",2);
m(4,"Public sympathy","What does the chapter say about public sympathy and Hosni Mubarak?","About 80% wanted him gone, but only about 1% took to the streets",["About 80% of Egyptians marched in the streets in 2011 against him","Only 1% of Egyptians wanted him gone, and they all marched","Polls showed overwhelming support for him until the day he left"],"Successful movements likely enjoyed overwhelming public sympathy even when few actively participated.",3);
m(4,"Snapshot","What is the third caveat about the way the rule was calculated?","It captures participation at one peak event, not how it builds",["It counts only women's participation in the largest protests","It measures only the first day of each campaign","It ignores the repression that the movement faced"],"Momentum, which accounts for mass and velocity, might be a better predictor.",3);
m(4,"Rare events","From 1945 to 2014, how many resistance campaigns surpassed the 3.5% threshold?","18 of 389, less than 5%",["38 of 189, about 20%","118 of 389, about 30%","8 of 289, about 3%"],"So the finding is based on very rare events.",3);
m(4,"Brunei","Which case does the chapter present as an exception to the 3.5% rule?","Brunei in 1962: 4,000 people, 4% of the population, rose and failed",["Egypt in 2011, where the army removed a president within weeks","The Philippines in 1986, where tanks withdrew without firing","The Central African Republic in 1990–1993, which forced a resignation"],"Brunei had about ninety thousand people; the sultan, backed by the UK, put down the revolt in ten days.",3);
vf(4,"A movement that mobilizes 3.5% of the population will always win.",false,"False: the figure describes a historical tendency; it is not a guarantee.",1);
vf(4,"The chapter says social media are always an advantage for nonviolent dissidents.",false,"False: the chapter lists this among the myths; the same tools can be captured and used by regimes.",1);
vf(4,"Overreliance on any one tactic makes an uprising predictable.",true,"True: predictability means vulnerability.",1);

// ===================== Giddens & Sutton · 5. Exclusion, welfare theories and Esping-Andersen =====================
m(5,"Crime and exclusion","What trend in modern societies do Young (1998, 1999) and others link to crime?","A drift away from citizenship rights toward accepting the exclusion of some",["A rapid rise in police numbers in the poorest districts of cities","A growing trust in public institutions among young unemployed people","A fall in the cost of living that mostly benefits better-off people"],"Crime rates may reflect a growing number of people who do not feel valued by, or invested in, the society they live in.",3);
m(5,"Currie","According to Currie (1998), what leads some young people in the United States toward illegitimate means?","Seductive consumer goods and shrinking labor-market opportunities",["Strong family traditions of crime in poor neighborhoods","Unlimited access to cheap credit and to consumer goods","Excessive public welfare that discourages young people from working"],"Economic restructuring led to a profound sense of relative deprivation and a willingness to turn to illegitimate means of sustaining a desired lifestyle.",2);
m(5,"Intolerant of work","What does McAuley mean when he says the dominant explanation for persistent youth crime is that some communities are “intolerant of work”?","Exclusion creates poor communities where some turn to crime",["Young people refuse to accept any job that is offered to them","Local employers refuse to hire anyone from the neighborhood","Unions block new work in the poorest areas of the region"],"McAuley summarizes this explanation but his findings contradict it: most young people he spoke to still valued work.",3);
m(5,"Exclusion at the top","What does “social exclusion at the top” mean?","A minority at the top opting out of mainstream institutions",["Wealthy people being barred from voting in national elections","The wealthy facing discrimination in public services","Rich people being excluded from every public position"],"Elite exclusion means a minority at the very top can opt out of mainstream institutions by merit of their affluence, influence and connections.",2);
m(5,"Why it matters","Why does the box say exclusion at the top is detrimental to an integrated society?","A closed private realm for the elite undermines solidarity and cohesion",["It makes the poor richer through the money the elite spends","It lowers taxes for everyone because the rich pay for private services","It makes society more equal by separating the rich from the poor"],"Just as social exclusion at the bottom undermines solidarity and cohesion.",2);
m(5,"Welfare state","Which of these defines the welfare state in the chapter?","A system where the state offers services and benefits for basic needs",["A system in which the market provides all services to paying citizens","A system of voluntary charities funded by private donations","A system of mutual insurance funds run by employers and unions"],"An important role is managing the risks people face over the course of their lives: sickness, disability, job loss and old age.",1);
m(5,"Tax in Sweden","In 2005, tax revenues in Sweden represented about what share of GDP?","51.1%",["34.7%","26.8%","37.2%"],"Belgium 45.4%, Austria 49.7%, the UK 37.2% (2006), Germany 34.7% and the USA 26.8% (OECD 2006).",2);
m(5,"Marxists","How do Marxists explain the welfare state?","As sustaining capitalism by producing a healthy, educated workforce",["As a first step toward communism and the end of private property","As a gift from the ruling class to buy off the working class","As the result of democratic voting by a working-class majority"],"Marxists see welfare as necessary to sustain a capitalist market-based society by ensuring a healthy, well-educated workforce.",2);
m(5,"Functionalists","How do functionalists explain the welfare state?","As helping integrate society in an orderly way under industrialization",["As an instrument of class struggle used by the ruling class","As a market failure that governments should gradually reverse","As a religious duty that churches should enforce"],"The ideas of T. H. Marshall and Gøsta Esping-Andersen have been particularly influential.",2);
m(5,"Marshall's stages","What are Marshall's three key stages of citizenship in Britain, in order?","Civil rights, then political rights, then social rights",["Political rights, then civil rights, then social rights","Social rights, then civil rights, then political rights","Civil rights, then social rights, then political rights"],"Civil in the eighteenth century, political in the nineteenth, social in the twentieth.",2);
m(5,"Optimistic","Why is Marshall's account often described as an optimistic one?","It sees a growing range of rights advancing equality for all",["It predicts the gradual end of the welfare state in Britain","It treats rights as a gift from the rich to the poor","It focuses mainly on conflict between social classes over rights"],"Social citizenship rights advance the ideal of equality for all.",2);
m(5,"Critique 1","What is the “immediate problem” with Marshall's explanation, according to critics?","It is based on a single case study, Britain",["It ignores the political rights obtained in the nineteenth century","It uses only economic data from the post-war period","It leaves out the role of the state in providing welfare"],"Critics (Turner 1990) show that his evolutionary approach cannot be applied to other cases such as Sweden, France or Germany.",2);
m(5,"Critique 2","What do critics say about Marshall's “evolutionary” explanation?","It describes how rights developed but not why they did",["It explains why rights developed in every country in the world","It rejects the idea that social rights ever existed in Britain","It assumes that rights can be reversed once they are granted"],"It does not explain how, say, civil rights lead inevitably to political and then social rights.",3);
m(5,"Critique 3","Why does globalization make Marshall's theory seem outdated, according to critics?","It assumes citizenship grows from a nation-state's internal dynamics",["It focuses on class struggle rather than on state institutions","It ignores economic growth after the Second World War","It ignores the role of religion in shaping rights"],"Sociologists are now more sensitive to relationships and influences across the world's societies.",3);
m(5,"Roll back","What does the “roll back” of welfare provision since the 1970s challenge in Marshall's thesis?","The idea of ever-growing rights, since welfare support may be restricted",["The idea that rights apply equally to all citizens everywhere","The idea that taxes should stay low to fund the state","The idea that citizens should vote in national elections"],"This does not appear to fit his historical thesis.",3);
m(5,"Environmental citizenship","What new type of citizenship does the chapter say seems to be emerging?","Environmental citizenship: rights and duties toward nature",["Digital citizenship, based on rights in online spaces","Economic citizenship, based on the right to a fair wage","Cultural citizenship, based on rights to a shared heritage"],"Cited are M. J. Smith (1998) and Dobson and Bell (2006); the concept is discussed in chapter 5.",2);
m(5,"Esping-Andersen","Which book presents Gøsta Esping-Andersen's typology of welfare regimes (1990)?","The Three Worlds of Welfare Capitalism",["The Welfare State in Crisis","The Great Transformation","Capital in the Twenty-First Century"],"He is a Danish sociologist; his work responds to criticism of Marshall's general evolutionary perspective.",1);
m(5,"Decommodification","What does “decommodification” mean?","How far welfare services are free from the market",["How far welfare benefits are taxed back from recipients","How far workers are paid according to their output","How far a country exports its public services abroad"],"With high decommodification, welfare is provided publicly and is not linked to income or economic resources.",1);
m(5,"Social democratic","Which statement describes Esping-Andersen's social democratic regime?","Highly decommodified: state-subsidized services for all citizens",["Mainly market-based, with means-tested help for the very needy","Benefits that depend on position and loyalty to the state","Charity-based help provided mostly by churches and families"],"Sweden and Norway are examples.",1);
m(5,"Conservative-corporatist","In the conservative-corporatist regime, what does the amount of benefit a citizen is entitled to depend on?","Their position in society",["Their income alone","Their age alone","Their nationality alone"],"Typical states: France and Germany. The aim is social stability, strong families and loyalty to the state rather than eliminating inequalities.",2);
m(5,"Liberal regime","Which country is the best example of a liberal welfare regime?","The USA",["Sweden","Germany","France"],"Welfare is highly commodified and sold through the market; means-tested benefits are available to the very needy but become highly stigmatized.",1);
m(5,"Why stigma","Why do means-tested benefits become stigmatized in the liberal regime?","Most people are expected to buy their welfare on the market",["They are paid only in cash and not in kind to recipients","They are provided mainly by churches and local charities","They are universal and available to all citizens"],"Means-tested benefits are for the very needy, so receiving them marks people out; most people are expected to buy welfare through the market.",3);
m(5,"UK shift","What shift makes the UK an interesting case study?","From social democratic toward liberal, with more commodification",["From liberal toward conservative-corporatist, with less market","From conservative-corporatist toward social democratic","From universal toward insurance-based and employer-run"],"It was closer to a social democratic model, but reforms since the 1970s brought it much closer to a liberal regime with higher commodification.",2);
vf(5,"Esping-Andersen's typology distinguishes three welfare regimes: social democratic, conservative-corporatist and liberal.",true,"True: this is his three-part typology of Western welfare systems.",1);
vf(5,"Marshall's three stages of citizenship are civil, political and social rights, obtained in that order.",true,"True: eighteenth, nineteenth and twentieth centuries respectively.",1);
vf(5,"In a commodified welfare system, benefits are provided publicly regardless of a person's income.",false,"False: that describes high decommodification. In a commodified system welfare is sold on the market.",1);

// ===================== Giddens & Sutton · 6. UK welfare reform and remodelling =====================
m(6,"Hung parliament","What was the result of the 2010 UK general election?","A hung parliament, so a Conservative–Liberal Democrat coalition",["A Labour majority government led by a new leader","A Conservative majority government with a small majority","A Labour–Liberal Democrat coalition after weeks of talks"],"The coalition's agenda put the reduction of government debt at the top, in part by fundamentally reforming public services and welfare provision.",1);
m(6,"Double crisis","What does Taylor-Gooby (2013) argue the UK welfare state faces?","Harsh spending cuts plus restructuring that fragments services",["A crisis of low taxes and an excess of public revenue","A crisis of low unemployment and wage inflation","A crisis of rising public spending on universal benefits"],"The result is fragmentation of services and increasing private provision throughout the public sector.",3);
m(6,"Work should pay","What was the central idea underpinning the coalition's reforms?","That “work should pay”: people better off working than on benefits",["That benefits should be paid at the same level to everyone","That work should be compulsory for every adult claimant","That taxes on employers should rise to fund new benefits"],"The Secretary of State for Work and Pensions, Iain Duncan Smith, had been working on reform for years.",1);
m(6,"Duncan Smith","Which think tank did Iain Duncan Smith found in 2004?","The Centre for Social Justice",["The Institute for Fiscal Studies","The Resolution Foundation","The Fabian Society"],"One influential CSJ publication, Dynamic Benefits (2009), helped shape the coalition's welfare policy.",2);
m(6,"Universal Credit","What was the aim of Universal Credit?","To reward work by cutting benefits gradually as earnings rise",["To abolish benefits for people who have any income from work","To give every citizen exactly the same income","To tax benefits at a flat rate of 100% for earners"],"It was to be phased in from 2013 for new claimants, replacing six income-related benefits.",2);
m(6,"Benefit cap","What did the benefit cap introduced under the Welfare Reform Act (2012) do?","Capped the total welfare a household could receive at £26,000 a year",["Capped rent at £26,000 a year for every household","Cut child benefit to £26,000 a year per child","Capped pensions at £26,000 a year for new claimants"],"It aimed to ensure households cannot receive more than the median after-tax earnings of those in work.",1);
m(6,"Cap's flaw","What does the chapter say is a problem with the benefit cap?","It ignores the different cost of rent and living, notably in London",["It is set too high for most households to reach","It applies only to pensioners living in council housing","It applies only to people over fifty who have no job"],"Regional differences in cost of living are ignored.",2);
m(6,"Child benefit","What happens to child benefit for families where anyone earns over £50,000?","It would be effectively “reclaimed” through income tax",["It would be doubled for families under that threshold","It would continue to be paid unchanged to every family","It would be abolished for all families from 2013"],"The chapter says this ends the universality of this longstanding benefit.",2);
m(6,"PIP","What replaced Disability Living Allowance from 2013?","The Personal Independence Payment (PIP)",["Universal Credit, which also replaced six benefits","Child benefit, paid to every family","The national living wage for disabled workers"],"PIPs remain non-means tested, but recipients face new “objective assessments” of their individual needs.",1);
m(6,"Bedroom tax","What was the controversial housing benefit change for council and housing association tenants?","A cut if tenants had “too many” bedrooms: the “bedroom tax”",["A cap on social rents at 30% of the tenant's income","Rent set at market level for all council tenants","A rise in rent for every tenant living alone"],"Critics and the Labour opposition dubbed it an unfair “bedroom tax”; the government argued social tenants with spare bedrooms were effectively paid a “spare room subsidy”.",1);
m(6,"Bedroom amounts","By how much was the housing benefit reduced for one and for two or more unused bedrooms?","14% for one bedroom and 25% for two or more",["25% for one bedroom and 14% for two or more","10% for one and 20% for two or more","14% for one and 14% for two or more"],"The benefit is reduced by 14% for one unused bedroom and by 25% for two or more.",2);
m(6,"Who suffers","Which groups did critics say would be particularly severely hit by the bedroom tax?","Older people and disabled people needing space for carers",["Single young people sharing flats in the big cities","Home owners with a mortgage who rent out a spare bedroom","Private tenants in expensive areas such as inner London"],"Spare rooms are used intermittently or for equipment and overnight stays by carers.",2);
m(6,"2015 election","What did the 2015 UK general election result mean for welfare reform?","A Conservative majority kept the coalition's welfare reform going",["A reversal of all the cuts made since 2010","A return to universal benefits for all families","A Labour government pledging to raise the benefit cap"],"It came to office with a plan to cut a further £12 billion from the welfare budget.",2);
m(6,"Thatcher","Which policy does the chapter say shows a clear line of continuity with Margaret Thatcher's privatization agenda?","Letting housing association tenants buy below market value",["Nationalizing the railways and the energy companies","Raising the minimum wage faster than inflation","Increasing child benefit for large families"],"The 2015 Conservative government let housing association tenants buy at below market value and forced councils to sell their most valuable homes.",3);
m(6,"Breadwinners","For what kind of society were modern, post-1945 welfare states created, according to the chapter?","Reconstruction, with men as breadwinners on a “family wage”",["Mass unemployment, with women seen as the main earners","Rapid immigration, with workers on temporary contracts","A mainly agricultural economy, with families on small farms"],"In the twenty-first century European societies have moved far from such conditions.",2);
m(6,"Hemerijck","How many main socio-economic changes does Hemerijck (2013) argue are pushing governments to look for a new welfare model?","Five: exogenous, endogenous, historical, supranational, political",["Three: economic, social and political, in that order","Four: demographic, economic, social and cultural","Seven: one for each decade since the post-war settlement"],"The five elements together produce a growing push for fundamental reform.",2);
m(6,"Endogenous","Which of these is an “endogenous” factor?","The shift away from manufacturing toward services",["Intensified competition from emerging economies","The supranational institutions of the European Union","The decline in party loyalty among voters"],"Others: the “feminization” of work, high-skill labor markets, fragmented employment relations, increasing life expectancy and ageing populations.",2);
m(6,"Old social risks","What does the “historical legacy of 'old social risks'” mean?","Large sums still go to unemployment, disability and pensions",["Welfare states no longer cover any of the old risks","Governments now spend nothing on pensions or sickness","New risks are fully covered by spending on the old ones"],"That limits the resources available in the era of austerity politics.",3);
m(6,"Semi-sovereign","What does Ferrera (2005) mean by saying welfare states have become “semi-sovereign”?","EU institutions also limit national states' ability to manage demands",["They answer mainly to regional and local governments","They are now privately owned by large insurance firms","They can no longer collect any taxes from citizens"],"This is the supranational factor.",3);
m(6,"Political challenges","Which political changes does the chapter list among the challenges to welfare states?","Declining party loyalty, electoral volatility and anti-EU feeling",["Rising party loyalty and very stable electoral politics","Growing support for the EU and for more immigration","A decline in every kind of social movement and protest"],"They were significant in the UK's 2016 referendum decision to leave the EU.",2);
m(6,"Resilience","Why have welfare states proved remarkably resilient to fundamental change (Pierson 2011)?","Welfare policy creates interest communities defending the status quo",["Governments have always wanted to keep it as it is","The European Union legally requires member states to keep it","Taxes are low enough that nobody wants change"],"A kind of in-built inertia.",3);
m(6,"Scroungers","What does the post-2008 debt crisis discourse do, according to the chapter?","Demonizes benefit “scroungers” and favors private provision",["Praises state welfare as a source of economic growth","Promotes public provision over private markets","Calls for higher benefits and wider eligibility"],"Post-2008 debt crises and declining revenues created conditions for a discourse that overtly criticizes state welfare, demonizes benefit “scroungers”, privileges private over public provision and promotes rolling back state involvement.",2);
m(6,"Age of responsibility","What does Lister (2011) mean by an “age of responsibility”?","Policy pushes the poorest to act as “responsible” citizens",["Citizens are freed from all duties toward the state","The state takes over responsibility for every risk","Responsibility passes from citizens to firms and employers"],"Lister argues we have entered an “age of responsibility” in which social policy increasingly pushes the poorest to feel and fulfil their obligations.",3);
m(6,"Rolling back","What do Taylor-Gooby and Stoker (2011) call unprecedented about the direction countries such as the UK are moving in?","Rolling back the state below the US level of intervention",["Raising state intervention above the level found in Sweden","Merging national welfare states into a single European one","Replacing public pensions with a universal basic income"],"“Rolling back the state to a level of intervention below that in the United States — something which is unprecedented.”",3);
vf(6,"The coalition government formed after the 2010 UK election was made up of the Conservatives and Labour.",false,"False: it was a coalition of Conservatives and Liberal Democrats.",1);
vf(6,"Universal Credit was designed to replace six income-related benefits for new claimants from 2013.",true,"True: it was to be phased in from 2013 and to simplify the system.",1);
vf(6,"The chapter says welfare states have proved remarkably resilient to fundamental change despite decades of reform pressure.",true,"True: it attributes this to interest communities that defend the status quo.",1);

